# Ghostnector verification configuration (optional).
#
# Copy this file to /etc/ghostnector/core.env and edit it, then:
#     sudo systemctl restart ghostnector-core
#
# `Protected` is only ever claimed from evidence: with nothing configured here, the daemon reports
# `protected, but unverified` and says why. Configure at least the UDP and HTTP checks to let it
# conclude. All three destinations must be OUTSIDE the local network, or the local-network
# exception cannot be verified and `connect --lan` is refused with an explanation (D-26).
#
#   --udp-check <ADDR:PORT>       an endpoint that answers a UDP datagram if one reaches it
#   --check-url <http://ADDR/>    an endpoint that answers 200 with the address it sees in the body
#   --canary <NAME@ADDR>          a name that should resolve to one address, through --canary-resolver
#   --canary-resolver <ADDR:PORT> the resolver the canary is asked of (usually 127.0.0.1:53)
#   --verify-interval <SECONDS>   how often to check (default 300)
#   --verify-stale-after <SECONDS> when a passing result stops being recent (default 900)
#   --verify-timeout <SECONDS>    how long one check may take (default 10)
#
# Example (replace the addresses with endpoints you control):
#GHOSTNECTOR_VERIFY=--udp-check 203.0.113.10:9999 --check-url http://203.0.113.10/ --canary canary.example@203.0.113.9 --canary-resolver 127.0.0.1:53
